Relevant tables & columns (only those needed)

citibike_trips  
• starttime / stoptime : ride date‑time – filter to 2016, compute duration if needed  
• tripduration        : bike ride length in seconds – use AVG for each route  
• start_station_id, end_station_id : define a “route”  
• start_station_name  : name we finally have to output  
• start_station_latitude / start_station_longitude : origin coordinates  
• end_station_latitude / end_station_longitude     : destination coordinates  

tlc_yellow_trips_2016  
• pickup_datetime / dropoff_datetime : trip date‑time – restrict to 2016 and compute duration  
• pickup_latitude  / pickup_longitude  : taxi origin coordinates  
• dropoff_latitude / dropoff_longitude : taxi destination coordinates  

Typical filters / conditions that will be used  
• EXTRACT(YEAR  FROM starttime) = 2016 (bike)  
• EXTRACT(YEAR  FROM pickup_datetime) = 2016 (taxis)  – table already 2016 but keep for clarity  
• pickup_latitude  IS NOT NULL AND dropoff_latitude  IS NOT NULL (same for longitude)  
• ROUND(coord , 3) to aggregate to the same 0.001‑degree (~110 m) grid as required in the question  

Below are ten exploratory → analytical SQL queries (all SELECT‑only, each ends with LIMIT 20) that gradually build toward the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at Citi Bike trips in 2016
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM starttime)=2016
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Show distinct start/end station pairs in 2016 with a small sample of their counts
SELECT start_station_id,
       end_station_id,
       COUNT(*) AS rides_2016
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM starttime)=2016
GROUP BY 1,2
ORDER BY rides_2016 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Add station names & keep top‑20 Citi Bike routes by volume in 2016
SELECT start_station_id,
       end_station_id,
       ANY_VALUE(start_station_name)  AS start_name,
       ANY_VALUE(end_station_name)    AS end_name,
       COUNT(*)                       AS trips
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM starttime)=2016
GROUP BY 1,2
ORDER BY trips DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. For those same top‑20 routes calculate their rounded (3‑dec) origin / destination coordinates
SELECT start_station_id,
       end_station_id,
       ROUND(ANY_VALUE(start_station_latitude ),3) AS start_lat_3d,
       ROUND(ANY_VALUE(start_station_longitude),3) AS start_lon_3d,
       ROUND(ANY_VALUE(end_station_latitude   ),3) AS end_lat_3d,
       ROUND(ANY_VALUE(end_station_longitude  ),3) AS end_lon_3d
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM starttime)=2016
GROUP BY 1,2
ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Compute average Citi Bike duration (in minutes) for the 20 busiest routes
SELECT start_station_id,
       end_station_id,
       AVG(tripduration)/60.0 AS avg_bike_min
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM starttime)=2016
GROUP BY 1,2
ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Pull yellow‑taxi trips whose rounded pick‑up / drop‑off coordinates match ANY of those 20 routes
SELECT ROUND(pickup_latitude ,3)  AS pu_lat_3d,
       ROUND(pickup_longitude,3)  AS pu_lon_3d,
       ROUND(dropoff_latitude ,3) AS do_lat_3d,
       ROUND(dropoff_longitude,3) AS do_lon_3d,
       TIMESTAMP_DIFF(dropoff_datetime, pickup_datetime, SECOND)/60.0 AS ride_min
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tlc_yellow_trips_2016`
WHERE pickup_latitude  IS NOT NULL
  AND dropoff_latitude IS NOT NULL
  AND pickup_longitude IS NOT NULL
  AND dropoff_longitude IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Average yellow‑taxi duration for each rounded origin/destination combination
SELECT ROUND(pickup_latitude ,3)  AS pu_lat_3d,
       ROUND(pickup_longitude,3)  AS pu_lon_3d,
       ROUND(dropoff_latitude ,3) AS do_lat_3d,
       ROUND(dropoff_longitude,3) AS do_lon_3d,
       AVG( TIMESTAMP_DIFF(dropoff_datetime, pickup_datetime, SECOND) )/60.0 AS avg_taxi_min
FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tlc_yellow_trips_2016`
WHERE pickup_latitude  IS NOT NULL
  AND dropoff_latitude IS NOT NULL
  AND pickup_longitude IS NOT NULL
  AND dropoff_longitude IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY 1,2,3,4
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Join top‑20 Citi Bike routes (rounded coords) with taxi averages & keep those where bikes are faster
WITH bike AS (
  SELECT start_station_id,
         end_station_id,
         ANY_VALUE(start_station_name)  AS start_name,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(start_station_latitude ),3) AS pu_lat_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(start_station_longitude),3) AS pu_lon_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(end_station_latitude   ),3) AS do_lat_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(end_station_longitude  ),3) AS do_lon_3d,
         AVG(tripduration)/60.0                    AS avg_bike_min
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM starttime)=2016
  GROUP BY 1,2
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 20
),
taxi AS (
  SELECT ROUND(pickup_latitude ,3)  AS pu_lat_3d,
         ROUND(pickup_longitude,3)  AS pu_lon_3d,
         ROUND(dropoff_latitude ,3) AS do_lat_3d,
         ROUND(dropoff_longitude,3) AS do_lon_3d,
         AVG(TIMESTAMP_DIFF(dropoff_datetime, pickup_datetime, SECOND))/60.0 AS avg_taxi_min
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tlc_yellow_trips_2016`
  WHERE pickup_latitude  IS NOT NULL
    AND dropoff_latitude IS NOT NULL
    AND pickup_longitude IS NOT NULL
    AND dropoff_longitude IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY 1,2,3,4
)
SELECT b.start_station_id,
       b.end_station_id,
       b.start_name,
       b.avg_bike_min,
       t.avg_taxi_min
FROM bike b
JOIN taxi t
  ON b.pu_lat_3d = t.pu_lat_3d
 AND b.pu_lon_3d = t.pu_lon_3d
 AND b.do_lat_3d = t.do_lat_3d
 AND b.do_lon_3d = t.do_lon_3d
WHERE b.avg_bike_min < t.avg_taxi_min   -- bikes are faster
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Among those “bike‑faster” routes, rank by (highest) average bike duration
WITH bike AS (
  SELECT start_station_id,
         end_station_id,
         ANY_VALUE(start_station_name)  AS start_name,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(start_station_latitude ),3) AS pu_lat_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(start_station_longitude),3) AS pu_lon_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(end_station_latitude   ),3) AS do_lat_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(end_station_longitude  ),3) AS do_lon_3d,
         AVG(tripduration)/60.0                    AS avg_bike_min
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM starttime)=2016
  GROUP BY 1,2
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 20
),
taxi AS (
  SELECT ROUND(pickup_latitude ,3)  AS pu_lat_3d,
         ROUND(pickup_longitude,3)  AS pu_lon_3d,
         ROUND(dropoff_latitude ,3) AS do_lat_3d,
         ROUND(dropoff_longitude,3) AS do_lon_3d,
         AVG(TIMESTAMP_DIFF(dropoff_datetime, pickup_datetime, SECOND))/60.0 AS avg_taxi_min
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tlc_yellow_trips_2016`
  WHERE pickup_latitude  IS NOT NULL
    AND dropoff_latitude IS NOT NULL
    AND pickup_longitude IS NOT NULL
    AND dropoff_longitude IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY 1,2,3,4
)
SELECT b.start_name,
       b.avg_bike_min,
       t.avg_taxi_min
FROM bike b
JOIN taxi t
  ON b.pu_lat_3d = t.pu_lat_3d
 AND b.pu_lon_3d = t.pu_lon_3d
 AND b.do_lat_3d = t.do_lat_3d
 AND b.do_lon_3d = t.do_lon_3d
WHERE b.avg_bike_min < t.avg_taxi_min
ORDER BY b.avg_bike_min DESC          -- longest bike time but still faster than taxi
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL answer: start‑station name of the route (among top‑20 busiest) that is faster than taxis
--     yet has the longest average bike duration
WITH bike AS (
  SELECT start_station_id,
         end_station_id,
         ANY_VALUE(start_station_name)  AS start_name,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(start_station_latitude ),3) AS pu_lat_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(start_station_longitude),3) AS pu_lon_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(end_station_latitude   ),3) AS do_lat_3d,
         ROUND(ANY_VALUE(end_station_longitude  ),3) AS do_lon_3d,
         AVG(tripduration)/60.0                    AS avg_bike_min
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.citibike_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM starttime)=2016
  GROUP BY 1,2
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 20
),
taxi AS (
  SELECT ROUND(pickup_latitude ,3)  AS pu_lat_3d,
         ROUND(pickup_longitude,3)  AS pu_lon_3d,
         ROUND(dropoff_latitude ,3) AS do_lat_3d,
         ROUND(dropoff_longitude,3) AS do_lon_3d,
         AVG(TIMESTAMP_DIFF(dropoff_datetime, pickup_datetime, SECOND))/60.0 AS avg_taxi_min
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.new_york.tlc_yellow_trips_2016`
  WHERE pickup_latitude  IS NOT NULL
    AND dropoff_latitude IS NOT NULL
    AND pickup_longitude IS NOT NULL
    AND dropoff_longitude IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY 1,2,3,4
)
SELECT b.start_name
FROM bike b
JOIN taxi t
  ON b.pu_lat_3d = t.pu_lat_3d
 AND b.pu_lon_3d = t.pu_lon_3d
 AND b.do_lat_3d = t.do_lat_3d
 AND b.do_lon_3d = t.do_lon_3d
WHERE b.avg_bike_min < t.avg_taxi_min
ORDER BY b.avg_bike_min DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

Running the final query will return exactly one value:  
the start station name of the busiest‑20 Citi Bike route in 2016 that is still quicker than yellow taxis yet has the longest average cycling time.
